data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1793534310257,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16 at 13:32 UTC; the command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part returning to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water to the command module; the Lunar Module had descent and ascent stages.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module\",\"Service Module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 culminated the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon before the decade's end; the program overcame the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Stepping onto the surface, Armstrong declared: \\\"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years afterwards.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"United States\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth; Alan Shepard followed on May 5, 1961 with a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\",\"Sputnik crisis\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the project would be more impressive and important than any other in this period, but also very difficult and expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing larger liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"The chunk says that a Moon landing would be an entire nation's achievement, since all must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University's football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said outer space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards are hostile to all.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued that the conquest of space deserves the best of all mankind and its chance for peaceful cooperation may never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answered why choose the Moon by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, ending with 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"the Moon\",\"Atlantic\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy stated the nation chooses to go to the Moon this decade because the goal is hard, to organize and measure the best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition, and mathematician Norbert Wiener dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing with Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon expedition again at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the idea was abandoned after his death.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"Moon\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"A space rendezvous is an orbital maneuver in which two spacecraft navigate through space and meet up.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, the service module, and the lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had two stages: a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"Technologies and techniques required for Apollo were developed by Project Gemini, and NASA used semiconductor advances like MOSFETs and silicon IC chips.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"Apollo 7 in October 1968 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"Apollo 8 in December 1968 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 9 in March 1969 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit, and Apollo 10 in May 1969 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, preparations were complete for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module\",\"Service Module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"the Moon\",\"Atlantic\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"7e4b0182a334e0567e2d4dc37e40f4d4a68e8effef7ed3d37305abaf09d62fac"}}

